<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leopold I of Belgium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a>–<a href="/years/1865">1865</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leopold I was the first king of the Belgians, reigning from 1831 to 1865. A prince of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>, he had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a>, heir to the British throne, who died in 1817, and he declined the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in 1830. Chosen by the Belgian National Congress after the revolution of 1830, he ruled as a constitutional monarch and secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s independence and neutrality. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Leopold II</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Leopold I of Belgium" width="320" height="562" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicaise de Keyser · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NICAISE_Leopold_ANV.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Leopold was born on 16 December 1790 at Ehrenburg Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, the youngest surviving son of Francis, Duke of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a>. His eldest brother, Ernest I, founded the dynasty of Saxe-Coburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gotha">Gotha</a> and was the father of Prince Albert. Leopold held Russian military rank from the age of six, fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and in 1814 went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with Emperor Alexander.</p><p>On 2 May 1816 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte of Wales</a>, the only legitimate child of the Prince Regent. He became a British citizen and a Field Marshal and Knight of the Garter that year. The marriage was happy but ended after a year and a half, when Charlotte died after delivering a stillborn son. As uncle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, Leopold arranged her marriage to his nephew Prince Albert. He refused the throne of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, offered under the London Protocol of February 1830, believing it too precarious.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Rebels in the southern provinces of the United Netherlands rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> rule at the end of August 1830, and a National Congress drew up a constitution for a popular monarchy. It refused any candidate from the Dutch house of Orange-Nassau, and the Great Powers wanted a neutral candidate. Leopold, the British-backed candidate, had connections with royal houses and was tied to no power believed to have territorial ambitions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. He took the oath as King of the Belgians on 21 July 1831, commemorated annually as Belgian National Day.</p><p>On 2 August 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> invaded, starting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-days-campaign">Ten Days' Campaign</a>. Leopold led a small force defending the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> and appealed for help, and the arrival of a French army forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> to withdraw. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of April 1839 the Netherlands recognised Belgian independence. In 1832 he married Louise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a>. He favoured the Liberals over the Catholics, although he refused to give up his Lutheran faith, and he helped fund <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>'s first railway in 1835. In 1848 about 6,000 armed émigrés of the Belgian Legion were stopped at Quiévrain and Risquons-Tout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>For his part in European affairs Leopold was called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He worked to repair Franco-British relations, arranging a meeting at the Château d'Eu in 1843 between Louis Philippe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. He died on 10 December 1865 of dysentery, aged 74, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-grand-duke-of-tuscany">Leopold II</a> succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leopold I of Belgium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A National Congress adopted a constitution on 7 February 1831 and, after the great powers vetoed a French prince, chose <b>Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</b>, a German prince who had been the widower of Britain’s heir Princess Charlotte.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coburg">Coburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Laeken, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.26,10.96&amp;year=1838" aria-label="Coburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leopold I of Belgium"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5595 5780h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5595 5780h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.26,10.96&amp;year=1838">What was here in 1838? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
